-      "what": "T-2764: on the camera the bow's lights and the loggia's plan place, everything this view shows ABOVE the principal lights' heads reads about 1.25-1.35 times taller than the model draws, while the principal and basement lights register: the loggia's column 73.9 px against 101 and its pedestal 29.5 against 38.5, and the bow's string course, whose foot the print draws about 56 px over the south light's head (y 134 to 78) where the render draws 45. Read inside the loggia with no camera but its heading, its column's height, its pedestal's and the south pair's width all read about 45 px to the metre, where the south pedestal's width reads 33 and the pairs' 2.5 m spacing about 32: the print draws the loggia about 1.35 times taller for its plan than it is built, against the 1888 plate, on whose level fits T-2763 lowered it to 0.88. Which picture errs here (this slide's optics, the bow's upper storey, or the loggia's plan) is T-2751's to rule. T-2830: the three tested by fit (tools/keith_1965_cause_fit.py; report docs/RESEARCH/photo-match/keith_house_1808_prairie/a18-gh-1808-porch-1965-cause-fit.json), all 29 of T-2764's coordinates fitted, the loggia's heights included. Not the slide's optics: a pitched camera (12-18 degrees), a radial distortion (k1 to its 1.5 bound), a free principal x and their combinations leave 5.3-5.7 px RMS (max 16-18), where a level camera given one factor on the loggia's heights fits at 3.45 (max 6.5) with k 1.29; the pedestal stands in the rows of the bow's sills and basement heads, which register, and no smooth map of the frame draws one 1.3 times the other. Not the bow: the principal heads at a free height (4.76 m) or every bow height scaled (0.89) leave 4.6-4.8 px (max 11), and the 1888 plate reads the bow's levels within 0.06 m. Not the loggia's plan as a size: its layout, its members or its pedestals scaled leave 5.6-5.7 px. As a place it fits (3.45 px), but only 2.0 m further toward the street and 2.7 m further south, which stands the deck's front on the public walk (the front is 4.44 m behind the street line) and the loggia in front of the bow, across the stair: refused. What is left is the loggia's own height, k 1.25-1.36 over any deck height, eye height or head reading tried. A third picture reads the same: on Nickel 1967's camera, fitted to the flat front's corners only, the as-built column draws 61 px from capital top to pedestal cap where the print reads 73 (luminance profiles at x 339-346), and against the bow's sill-to-string-course span beside it the column stands 1.30 times the model's proportion. So two late pictures stand the loggia about 1.3 times taller than T-2763 built it on the plate's level fits, with T-2725's reading of the plate against the front's own ruler (a 2.51 m column, 1.13) between them. Which the house is built to is T-2831's to rule, so this stays open.",
+      "what": "T-2764: on the camera the bow's lights and the loggia's plan place, everything this view shows ABOVE the principal lights' heads reads about 1.25-1.35 times taller than the model draws, while the principal and basement lights register: the loggia's column 73.9 px against 101 and its pedestal 29.5 against 38.5, and the bow's string course, whose foot the print draws about 56 px over the south light's head (y 134 to 78) where the render draws 45. Read inside the loggia with no camera but its heading, its column's height, its pedestal's and the south pair's width all read about 45 px to the metre, where the south pedestal's width reads 33 and the pairs' 2.5 m spacing about 32: the print draws the loggia about 1.35 times taller for its plan than it is built, against the 1888 plate, on whose level fits T-2763 lowered it to 0.88. Which picture errs here (this slide's optics, the bow's upper storey, or the loggia's plan) is T-2751's to rule. T-2830: the three tested by fit (tools/keith_1965_cause_fit.py; report docs/RESEARCH/photo-match/keith_house_1808_prairie/a18-gh-1808-porch-1965-cause-fit.json), all 29 of T-2764's coordinates fitted, the loggia's heights included. Not the slide's optics: a pitched camera (12-18 degrees), a radial distortion (k1 to its 1.5 bound), a free principal x and their combinations leave 5.3-5.7 px RMS (max 16-18), where a level camera given one factor on the loggia's heights fits at 3.45 (max 6.5) with k 1.29; the pedestal stands in the rows of the bow's sills and basement heads, which register, and no smooth map of the frame draws one 1.3 times the other. Not the bow: the principal heads at a free height (4.76 m) or every bow height scaled (0.89) leave 4.6-4.8 px (max 11), and the 1888 plate reads the bow's levels within 0.06 m. Not the loggia's plan as a size: its layout, its members or its pedestals scaled leave 5.6-5.7 px. As a place it fits (3.45 px), but only 2.0 m further toward the street and 2.7 m further south, which stands the deck's front on the public walk (the front is 4.44 m behind the street line) and the loggia in front of the bow, across the stair: refused. What is left is the loggia's own height, k 1.25-1.36 over any deck height, eye height or head reading tried. A third picture reads the same: on Nickel 1967's camera, fitted to the flat front's corners only, the as-built column draws 61 px from capital top to pedestal cap where the print reads 73 (luminance profiles at x 339-346), and against the bow's sill-to-string-course span beside it the column stands 1.30 times the model's proportion. So two late pictures stand the loggia about 1.3 times taller than T-2763 built it on the plate's level fits, with T-2725's reading of the plate against the front's own ruler (a 2.51 m column, 1.13) between them. Which the house is built to is T-2831's to rule, so this stays open. T-2831: ruled — the column stands at its built 2.2 m, and what this slide reads above the heads is its ruler. Its principal glass stops at 0.664 of the sill-to-string-course span down the -33 degree light (head y 134 between the sill at 244.5 and the course's foot at 78), 4.66 m: the middle light's head (4.70 m), the top lights over it blind, as Nickel 1967 shows them too (0.663). This camera pairs those heads with the 5.30 m head the 1888 plate glazes. Re-fitted with the heads at 4.70 m on T-2764's 29 coordinates, the bow's heads and sill sit within 1.6 px and the loggia's factor falls to k 1.15 (3.94 px), and with the loggia free to move in plan to k 1.04 (2.76 px), the move about 0.7-0.8 m south (dv -0.72 to -0.79; 2.84 px with k held at 1). Nickel 1967, its capital top re-read at the beam's soffit, stands the column at 0.994 of the built proportion on the bow's span, and the 1888 plate at 1.057. Still open, and T-2836's: this camera re-solved with the heads at 4.70 m, the loggia's place in plan ruled (the 1888 plate's south pair also stands about 30 px south of the model's), and the view re-rendered (docs/RESEARCH/photo-match/keith_house_1808_prairie/keith-loggia-column-rule.json).",
       "status": "open"
     },
